<?xml version="1.0" encoding="windows-1252"?>
<!-- Byte-for-byte Windows-1252, not UTF-8. Do not "fix" the encoding of this
     file: the bytes below in 0x80-0x9F are what makes it evidence. In UTF-8
     those positions are illegal continuation bytes, so a reader that ignores the
     declaration produces U+FFFD rather than mojibake, and the failure is visible
     rather than subtle.

     The .meta file claims charset=utf-8 on the HTTP header. That disagreement is
     deliberate and it is the common real-world one: a server default set once in
     1998 and never revisited, over a file the publisher generates correctly. The
     declaration has to win. -->
